WEBVTT

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:08.060
The social season begins with a brilliant party in the residence of Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:16.960
Captain Jeffrey T. Spaulding, famous explorer, on his return from Africa, will be the guest of honor.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:27.970
Roscoe V. Chandler, eminent artist, will exhibit his famous painting after the hunt at the same party.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:02:00.000
your best be on your toes as an honored guest tonight is one of those men who are being pleated by the smart men

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.840
We'll see that he gets what he deserves.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.860
Treat him as they do a king, in manner royal, like a subject to a king.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.820
You must be loyal, on this object you must have your heart set.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.660
We'll do nothing to get on his nerves.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.260
Again a mention, be on your toes, man.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.460
He craves attention, he's one of those men.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.160
Yes, sir, we will give him just what he deserves.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.280
We'll see that he gets what he deserves.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.530
Tell me, Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.010
I'd like to make some changes in the rooms.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:41.970
Very well, ma'am.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.310
We'll put Mr. Chandler in the blue suite

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.810
and Captain Spaulding, as an honorary guest,

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.850
in the green duplex with two bathrooms.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:49.770
Two, ma'am?

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.410
Yes, I think the captain would like to have two bathrooms.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:54.410
Don't you think?

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:58.270
If he just returned from Africa, he'll need two bathrooms.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.000
Mr. Roscoe.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Mr. Cusco V. Chandler.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.250
Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.490
Oh, Mr. Chandler.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.850
I'm so happy to see you.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.330
The famous Boregar, as I promised.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.670
Mr. Chandler, how can I show my gratitude to you?

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.670
- Oh, it's nothing. - Nothing?

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:24.020
You return from Europe with a masterpiece valued at £100,000.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.180
And instead of enjoying it yourself,

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.000
you authorize me to exhibit it at the party I give in honour of Captain Spalding.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.560
That's not what you call anything.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.900
Mrs. Rittenhouse...

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.300
Do you want to take him to the place where he has to be exhibited?

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.540
Very well, sir.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:41.390
Treat him very carefully, please.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.750
Mrs. Rittenhouse, consider him something.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.110
And it is nothing compared to what I would like to do for you.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.670
You are a very beautiful woman.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.750
Oh, no, Mr. Charles.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.550
Well, maybe you're wrong.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:54.960
What?

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.140
Oh, please don't go, Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.000
- I love you. - I know. Oh, Mr. Charles.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.900
- Mr. Chandler, be careful, you may... - Hello, mom.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.750
- Playing with the sisters? - Well, with your permission.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:12.200
- Have you seen Johnny Parker? - I'd like you to forget about him...

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.240
...and show more respect to Mr. Chandler.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.240
The bad thing about you is that you don't take seriously the way you behave in society.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:21.240
And what would you advise me, mom? Suicide?

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:24.160
I would advise you that...

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:27.170
- I'll talk later. - Mrs. Rittenhouse, Captain Spongy has just arrived.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.000
Oh, joy! My friends...

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.060
The brave Captain Spalding has just arrived.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:58.120
Mr. Horacio V. Jamison.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.000
Helper of El Campo del Río.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.910
Captain Spaulding.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:13.080
I come in representation of the captain who insists that I report the conditions

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.230
under which he will camp here. In one thing it is very rigid, he wants women

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:26.520
young and thin, as for the men he does not want to see them around him.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.000
Men have to be old, women have

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.170
the champagne cold, those are their conditions.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.200
- How much do I owe you? -

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:20.720
What? From Africa? What, 1.85? That's an offense, I told you not to bring me across Australia.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.930
You know it's all set up, you should have come directly through Boulevard Lincoln.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:26.810
Where do you come from with that outfit? And your registration, where is it?

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:28.010
- I don't trust you. - I'll take care of it.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.000
I'll take care of it later. Oh, you're one of the most beautiful women.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.000
...and that's not exaggerating at all.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.000
Captain Spaulding, the Rittenhouse mansion is at your disposal.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.000
I'm certainly grateful for this magnificent ovation.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
I want to see that competition, and now...

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.560
...I'd like to say a few words.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:43.730
Hello?

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.860
I must be going.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.750
I cannot say I came to say I must be going.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.750
I'm glad I came, but just to say I must be going.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:52.750
La, la.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.630
The nice things you must say...

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.450
...if you should go away...

00:06:56.450 --> 00:07:00.000
...you spoil this party...

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:23.650
I must be

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.090
I'd like to say that he's too modest to relate.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.980
The captain is a mild man.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:36.980
Sometimes he finds a trial.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.980
In fact, I'll emphasize mistrust.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:40.980
I never take a drink unless he--

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:41.980
Somebody's lying.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.980
Captain, he's a very mild man.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.380
If he hears anything obscene,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:48.270
he'll naturally repell it.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.270
I hate it, any joke I do.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:54.320
Unless it's told by someone who knows how to tell it.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.410
Captain, he's a very mild man.

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:00.000
He's a man who's more dangerous.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:11.440
the jungle where all the monkeys thrown up if I stay here I'll go nuts

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.000
I am very grateful for this display

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:16.110
diffusion and I want you to know, my friends, I am highly grateful for this diffusion deployment

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:30.000
and I want you to know, my friends, I am highly grateful

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.000
...by this magnificent fusion deployment.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.000
And I want you to know...

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.000
...Long live Captain Spaulding, the great explorer!

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Someone had to do it.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Captain Spaulding, it is a great honor to welcome you...

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.000
...to my modest home.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.000
Oh, it's not bad.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.000
It's not necessary to say that...

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.000
I think you're right. It's pretty bad.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.000
And by the way, it's one of the ugliest messes I've ever seen.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.000
But, Captain...

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.000
Where did you buy the wallpaper?

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:51.000
Well, I...

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.000
Put this on sale and what's the result?

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.000
The class of people who are used to it will not come.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.000
Why do you have people here who look like you?

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.080
I'm going to tell you what we're going to do.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:58.080
We'll put a poster out there.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.000
This house is going to have a new administration...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.140
We offer food for 75 cents, that will leave them with an eyesore.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.100
And when they have it, they will give us as much as we want.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.480
Now sign here a check worth 1,500 dollars.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.040
Now I want to tell you, ma'am, that with this insurance policy you have protected your little ones

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.840
and also protects your old age, which will arrive in two weeks.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.740
If I am an expert in horseback riding.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.960
And now, ma'am, the time has come. I have already said that...

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:19.760
- Captain Spaulding? - I have to talk to you.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.950
Captain Spaulding, for me, you are one of the bravest men of all time.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.690
Okay, that's right.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:30.000
In the dark forests of Africa, there has been no danger that you have not faced.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.280
- Is it worth that I don't smoke? - Intrepidly, you have opened new paths.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:38.280
Challenging the roars of the lions and the tantalum of the cannibals.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:42.870
- When do you say that? - Not once in those tiring months

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:47.050
you have taken a step back. Ignore the cowardice.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:51.050
- Fear has never been known. - Excuse me, a caterpillar.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.060
- Captain! Captain! - What's wrong with you?

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:57.060
- Put it here. It must have been the caterpillar that scared you.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:00.000
- Don't stand there. Bring some whiskey. - Bring something.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.600
Whisky! Whisky! Where's the whiskey?

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.570
It's in my black suitcase, in the corner to the right.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:13.760
Mr. Emanuel Ravelli.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:25.160
- How are you? - Nice to meet you.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.170
- Where's the dining room? - Oh, how rude!

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:30.000
Hey, I knew a guy who was just like you, and his name was...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.000
My name is Emanuele Ravelli. Are you his brother?

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.000
I'm Emanuele Ravelli.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.000
Are you Emanuele Ravelli?

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.000
I'm Emanuele Ravelli.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.830
I'm not shocked that you look alike, but I insist that there is a resemblance.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.830
Ha ha ha! You think I look alike?

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:43.830
If that's the case, it's a great advantage for both of you.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:48.070
Are you one of the musicians? Weren't you invited until tomorrow?

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:50.070
I couldn't come tomorrow. It's too early.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.020
You're lucky you didn't come yesterday.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.940
We were busy yesterday, but we charge the same.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.990
This is better than exploring.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:57.990
How much do you charge per hour?

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:00.000
We charge 10 dollars a month.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.000
I understand. And how much for not playing?

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.000
12 dollars an hour.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.800
Well, I'm in on that.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.390
Now, for rehearsing, we make a special price. 15 dollars an hour.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.390
- On the count? - No, on the act.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.760
- And how much do they charge for not rehearsing? - I couldn't calculate it.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:17.760
You see, if we don't rehearse, we don't act.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.920
And if we don't act, we lose money.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:23.230
How much do you want to roll for a precipice?

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:25.700
Just a compensation.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:27.780
I've been good.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.800
Well, fall down sometime.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:34.980
- We've cleaned it very well. - Well, let's see how we were.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:38.640
- Flat feet. - We didn't come here yesterday.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.650
- Remember? We didn't come here yesterday. - Yes, I remember.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.770
- It's 300 dollars. - They didn't come here yesterday. It's 300 dollars.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.870
- Yes, it's 300 dollars. - Well, that's reasonable. I accept it.

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:50.640
- We came here today. It's... - You owe us 100.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:55.620
I think I'm going to lose in the deal. We're leaving tomorrow. That could be worth...

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.590
- A million dollars. - Yes, that's fine by me, but I have a partner.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.000
- What? - A partner?

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.720
The Professor!

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:23.790
The door opened and a monkey came in.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:26.140
How are you?

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:42.960
Why don't you have any chocolate?

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.180
He's got everything.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.970
Hives, take the coat and the professor's hat.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:51.410
And come to fumigate.

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:55.030
Oh, what a horror! What a horror!

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:57.960
- But where do you think he is? In a pool? - Hey, you!

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:00.000
Don't you see that there are ladies in front? Who sent you?

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.460
- Let go of me, you bastard! What is this? - What are you doing here? Get out!

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.200
A little more to the right.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:24.170
That's better.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:26.170
See if the curtain is well hung.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:28.170
The lady will inspect it before you go to discover it.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:29.190
Hello, Hives.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:32.890
Mrs. Whitehead! What a surprise!

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:33.690
What do you mean?

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:36.290
Mrs. Grace, wow, you've grown so much in a year!

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:38.090
And you haven't lost a pound!

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:40.890
Unfortunately, no.

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:45.010
You know, Hives, I miss seeing you working here instead of seeing you at home.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:49.210
When you left for Europe, Mrs. Rittenhouse was kind enough to make me an offer.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:50.090
That's good.

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:54.310
By the way, Hives, what's the secret of that painting?

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:57.470
No, it's not a secret. It's after the Boregar hunt.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:58.470
The original?

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.000
Very original.

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:05.530
- Be careful. - How did you get it? Can you imagine?

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:09.040
- It's very valuable. - I think so. It's worth a fortune.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:14.000
It's as if we had given all the success of the season to Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:16.800
- But do you think we can do something? - We can shoot ourselves.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.760
I'd rather shoot Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:24.300
Hey, I have an idea. I copied that painting at the art school a year ago.

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:30.000
- Well, and? - I still don't understand.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.900
I'm going home and I bring the copy. We replace it with the original, and when they find out, what will happen?

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:39.820
An imitation, and I must admit it's pretty bad.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:41.820
Grace, you're great.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:44.890
I'll do what I said. I won't take long.

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.210
Okay. See you later.

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:56.320
Hyves, do you still feel that we have a strong bond?

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:58.300
Certainly, Mrs. Whitehead.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.000
Quite so.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.440
- As strong as to do me a favor? - Whatever it is.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:07.170
Well, let's suppose I ask him to take the Boregal.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.350
- What? He intends to... - Only temporarily.

00:17:09.350 --> 00:17:11.810
And to replace it for one of mine.

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:15.070
But I must consider that as a very special proposal.

00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:18.920
It is, Hives. But I still consider him as of the family of the Whitehead.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.780
Oh, you can count on me.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:28.610
My soul is yours, even when my body belongs to Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:30.000
Good, Hives!

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:33.520
Oh, Mrs. Whitehead.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:34.420
Arabella?

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:36.790
Let me introduce you to Mr. Parker.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:38.790
Oh, the parkers from Massachusetts?

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:39.740
Well, no.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.660
Then, the parkers from the South?

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.660
No, no, the parkers from the Central Park.

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.660
The banks and the trees.

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:48.660
I understand.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:50.640
Meow.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:54.430
Oh, I don't like that woman.

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:58.640
Well, don't worry. Come here, sit down and tell me who you like.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:00.000
What do you want to play with?

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.080
- Tell me, is it true that you love me? - Why don't you marry me and prove it?

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:12.670
- Marry me? With what? Last year I sold two paintings, one for 100 dollars and the other for 50.

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:18.720
Can we live with 150 dollars a year? - We won't need money. I have a

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:24.960
regular account in all the stores on Fifth Avenue. - Anyway, if I had more talent,

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:30.000
I would start a more profitable job. I have a cousin who earned 50,000 dollars in gold

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.600
Street last year. But I'm not going to marry your cousin. I'm crazy, I want to marry you.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:42.190
Hey, why don't you convince that chandler to take care of your portrait?

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:48.520
Chandler? What does he know about art? Better, the less you know, the easier it will be for you to sell it.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:58.930
They are upstairs, you can't miss them, you will find them right away. Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.330
Oh, Captain Spaulding, how do you do?

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.320
Tell me, are you alone?

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:09.520
Why, Captain? I don't understand.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:10.920
Don't you understand what it is to be alone?

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.000
Don't be an idiot or you'll be left alone.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:15.720
A hen like you trying to fool me.

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:21.030
- Mrs. Rittenhouse. - Yes?

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:23.080
Oh, sorry, sorry.

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:25.930
Sometimes you must have felt as excited as I did.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:30.000
- No, Captain. - Well, you will feel, Mrs. Rittenhouse.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:34.280
Since I've known her, I've been in awe of her.

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:36.300
Something is beating inside me.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.400
Oh, it hits me like lightning from the early jungle.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:41.580
There's something I want to ask you.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:43.530
What is it, Captain?

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:46.190
Do you want to wash a pair of socks for me?

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.190
But Captain, I'm surprised.

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:51.050
Maybe you are, but I've been imagining it for weeks.

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:53.050
It's my way of telling you that I love you, that's all.

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.050
- I love you, I love you. - Oh, yes, yes.

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:57.050
- There has never been a woman... - Excuse me.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:58.050
Excuse me.

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:00.000
- Am I inopportune? - Inopportune?

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.240
Just when I had her in the boat.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.610
Of course, she's not lucky.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:06.660
I think she's not lucky, excuse me.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:08.660
I speak in the present instead of the past.

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:10.660
Yes, we're in the past.

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:12.660
Now we live in bungalows.

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:14.660
We're in a mechanized age.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:17.680
Mrs. Whitehead, don't you know Captain Spaulding?

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.680
Of course I know him. How are you?

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:21.530
How are you?

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:23.530
I'm fine, thank you. And you, how are you?

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:24.530
And you, how are you?

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:25.960
Are we tied?

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:30.000
Hasn't anyone told you that you have beautiful eyes?

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.800
- No. - Well, you have them.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:39.090
And you too, say goodbye in a light that looks like a smile on your lips.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:43.650
It's true, I don't think I've ever seen four more beautiful eyes in my life.

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:45.300
Not to mention mine.

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:49.990
You two have it all, they are tall and short, thin and fat, blonde and brown.

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:52.400
Exactly what I like in the woman I yearn for.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.730
The three of us would make a great couple.

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:58.010
You have beauty, charm, money. You have money, right?

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:00.000
Because if you don't have it, we could separate.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.010
The Captain is charming, right?

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:04.010
I'm fascinated.

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:05.920
I'm also vaccinated.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:06.840
Right here.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.450
What do you think?

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:13.660
If I were Eugenio Niles, I could tell you what I actually think about the two.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:16.840
It's a good thing that this piece is representing us now.

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:17.940
They would know what is good.

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:22.510
Now I'm going to say my monologue.

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:27.080
What a bunch of fools.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:29.350
If they think I'm going to marry.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.360
It's strange how the wind blows tonight.

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:35.190
It has a terrifying voice, it reminds me of the fairy tales.

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:40.060
How happy I would be with either of them if they both left here.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:44.590
What do you say, girls? Do you want to marry me?

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:47.590
- But Captain, which of the two? - The two of us are going to get married.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:54.540
This is my party. Party, party, I'm talking about party.

00:21:54.900 --> 00:22:00.000
I've come here to a party and what happens? Nothing, not even an ice cream.

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:03.270
The gods contemplate them and laugh at them.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:07.130
This world would be better for children if their parents had to eat spinach.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:10.600
Well, what do you say, girls?

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.850
What do you say? Are we going to get married?

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:14.470
- All of us? - All of us.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:17.020
- That would be bigamy. - Yes, huge for me.

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:20.120
- Oh, how barbaric! - Huge for all of us. Let's be great for once.

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:22.020
I'm bothered by those marriage deals.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.420
A man and a woman were enough for our grandmothers.

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:27.910
But who's going to marry his grandmother? Nobody. Not even his grandfather.

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:30.000
Think, think about the honeymoon.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.150
and I won't allow any other woman to intervene in it.

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:34.110
Well, maybe one or two, but no man.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:35.510
Maybe I won't go either.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:38.400
Are you offering us a wedding in command?

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:40.900
It would have its advantages, you could live with your family

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.350
and I could live with your families.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:49.240
And you could sell brushes, the beginning of the end.

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:55.120
The gray days of yesterday illuminating the beautiful days of today.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:59.960
The noise of scary steps through the corridors full of mystery.

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:06.190
And in those corridors I see figures, strange figures, many figures.

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:12.990
steel 186, anaconda 74, french fries, American gum.

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:14.910
But what were we talking about?

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:19.520
Oh yes, we were talking about marriage. What have you thought? Do you really think we should get married?

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:25.000
I think marriage is the most noble of institutions.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.040
It is the foundation of the American home.

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:30.000
Yes, but it cannot be put in force. I was instituted in the

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:34.030
American town when our men were on the other side. While our

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:38.170
women were here. I will tell you that I have been waiting for the foot of this ladder for years,

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:43.750
a moment like this, ladies. But captain, where are you going? I'm sorry, I'm really sorry,

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:48.020
but we're going to have to postpone the wedding a few days, maybe a few years.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:54.660
Before I get married, I'm going to sow some oatmeal fields. How fun to be a captain.

00:23:54.660 --> 00:24:00.000
Yes, it's lovely. So this is the famous Boregal. It is a magnificent painting

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.530
- You haven't seen it, have you? - See it?

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:04.560
I was in Paris copying it for three days.

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:07.730
- Really? - Well, me and millions of artists.

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:13.640
- Oh, I bet your copy was the best of them all. - It wasn't bad at all.

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:16.640
- I'll show you one day, you'll see. - Hey.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.330
- What? - Mom's going to discover it tonight.

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:23.840
- Really? - Let's suppose...

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:27.620
Let's suppose that when she lifts the curtain, the Boregal isn't there.

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:30.000
Let's suppose that yours is there.

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:32.720
That my copy is on the site of the Boregar?

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:33.520
Exactly.

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:36.490
But Arabella, you're crazy. What would it be useful for me?

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:40.120
What? Look, they discover it.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:42.760
Chandler explains to the guests how magnificent it is.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:44.950
And they applaud with great enthusiasm.

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:47.280
Then we tell them that you are the one who painted it.

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.040
Everyone says that you are a great artist.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:51.160
You get six commissions for so many other paintings.

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:54.470
And we can get married and get divorced in no time.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:55.910
Yes, that would be great.

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:57.730
But what if they don't like my painting?

00:24:57.730 --> 00:24:59.280
What will happen?

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:00.000
It's not worth it.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:08.950
- Are you going to take that risk? - Oh, I can see that you are admiring my painting.

00:25:08.950 --> 00:25:10.860
- Yes, yes. - Your painting?

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:14.290
- Yes, my painting. - Well, it doesn't look like you.

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:18.690
Look, look, wait, I must have seen you somewhere, because your face seems very familiar to me.

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:22.090
It may be. I am one of the most famous men in America.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:24.690
The press constantly publishes my photography.

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:27.680
- Won't it be Salvador Dalí? - Oh, how crazy!

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:32.900
Wait a moment, if it's not Salvador Dalí, who are you?

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:36.900
Please, my name is Chander, Roscoe V. Chander.

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:38.480
Yes, but I don't care about the name.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:41.480
It's the place where I've seen you before, because your face looks familiar to me.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.300
Wait, let's see, have you ever been to Shinshin?

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:45.640
- Please! - No.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:48.340
I have it, I have it, come here, come here, in Pennsylvania.

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.340
- Leave me alone. - Don't leave, don't leave.

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:54.100
- Maybe you've been to Alcatraz. - Listen to me, I've never been to those places.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:56.560
- I spend most of my time in Europe. - Europe?

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:00.000
I don't have it anymore, I know, I know, it comes from Czechoslovakia.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.000
You're mistaken, I told you...

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:04.000
He's from Czechoslovakia!

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.000
Hey! Hey! Come here, come here!

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.000
Please, get out of there!

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.000
Look at his feet! He's from Czechoslovakia!

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.950
You're mistaken, I told you he's mistaken!

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:15.950
He's from Czechoslovakia, and I know who he is!

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:18.840
He's the one, the fish-seller from Czechoslovakia!

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:20.840
Don't be ridiculous, I told you he's mistaken!

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:22.840
Fine, I'll prove it, I'll prove it!

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:24.850
He had a birthmark, remember?

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:26.850
Please, what are you trying to do?

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:28.850
On the leg, on the foot, on the ear...

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:30.000
- In the ear! - Get out!

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.500
In the moustache! In the buttocks!

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:34.480
Here's the mark! Here's the mark!

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:36.470
Eide, the fisherman.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.470
That's right, I'm Eide, the fisherman.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:40.970
How did you become Roscoe V. Chandler?

00:26:40.970 --> 00:26:42.970
Look, how did you become an Italian?

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:45.240
What does it matter? I'm the one who asks.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.240
Listen, guys, don't tell anyone about this.

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:50.240
We can reach an agreement, an agreement,

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.240
that can make us mutually satisfied.

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:53.240
Go ahead, if you want.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:55.630
Let's say... 500 dollars.

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:57.130
500 dollars is little.

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:59.140
That's all the money I have on me. If you don't want that, I won't give you anything.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.000
500 dollars.

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:02.990
Listen, I'll tell you what we're going to do. We'll accept a payment.

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:05.910
I've made you a good offer, and that's all I'll do.

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:07.270
- Are we just going to take that out? - Yes.

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:10.740
Hey, the fisherman! Hey, the fisherman!

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:13.640
- Hey, the fisherman! Hey, the fisherman! - Please, guys!

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:16.100
- Silence, please! Please! - Hey, the fisherman!

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:18.050
Come here and listen to me.

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:20.990
I have a $5,000 check here, I'll give it to you as a gift.

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:23.470
- Hey, is it good? - Of course it's good.

00:27:23.470 --> 00:27:26.020
- Who's going to give me a fake check? - Myself.

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:27.550
Hey, look if it's good.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:36.060
All right, if you don't want the check, I won't give you anything else.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:37.650
- Are we just going to take that out? - Yes.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:46.000
♪ Eiviel Pescadero, Eiviel Pescadero, Eiviel Pescadero, Eiviel Pescadero, Eiviel Pescadero, Eiviel Pescadero, Eiviel Pescadero ♪

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:49.260
Give me my handkerchief!

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:51.570
My tie! Where is my tie?

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:55.410
You are a couple of thieves, that's it!

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:58.770
I have the tie, what do you have?

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:00.000
The birthmark!

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:05.860
- Hey, hey! What about your tie? - My tie?

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:08.860
What a great posture for a millionaire to go around with his neck undone!

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:11.860
- Do you wear sneakers? - Of course I wear sneakers!

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:14.750
Look! Oh, they took my sneakers!

00:28:14.750 --> 00:28:17.750
What a story! As if I were a schoolboy, huh? Do you wear socks?

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:20.990
- Of course I wear socks! - You wear socks!

00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:24.110
- They look very old to me. Whose are they? - They are mine!

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:27.120
For many years. Let me introduce myself. My name is Spaulding, Captain Spaulding.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:30.000
- I am Roscoe V. Chan. - No, no, Roscoe V. Chan.

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:34.710
- Have you heard about me? - Yes, I've heard about you for many years, Mr. Chandler.

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:36.710
And I'm tired of hearing about you.

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:40.920
And as it is natural, I have also heard about the great Captain Spaulding.

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:42.920
Very well, I have heard about you and you have heard about me.

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:44.920
And now you know the joke of the Irish.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:46.920
Oh, yes!

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:51.070
Well, since we have gotten into a joke, let's talk about business.

00:28:51.070 --> 00:28:53.070
My name is Spaulding, Captain Spaulding.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:55.070
- I am Roscoe V. Chandler. - I am Jeffrey T. Spaulding.

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:57.070
I bet you don't know what "T" means.

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.930
Uh... Thomas.

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:02.470
Edgar, you've come a long way.

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.220
He was close, and I think he's still there.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:06.220
Now, what I wanted to talk to you about, Mr. Chaddard.

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:08.840
What would you think about financing a scientific expedition?

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:10.640
Well, that's another question.

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:12.940
Another question? You know what a question is when you have it in front of you.

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:14.240
Congratulations, Mr. Chaddard.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:16.440
And this leads us directly to the starting point.

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:19.040
What do you think about financing a scientific expedition?

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:21.840
Do you have any special expeditions in mind?

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.840
Well, I'll tell you, I'm starting to get old and there's something I've always wanted to do before I became one.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.240
- What is it? - Retire.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:29.700
Would you be interested in a proposal of that kind?

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:30.000
I always say:

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.000
I have always had the idea that my retirement would be the greatest contribution that science would make since the world is a world.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:38.000
This is your opportunity, Mr. Chandler, when I think about what you have done for this country.

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:40.000
And by the way, what have you done for this country?

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:43.890
Well, I've always tried to do what I could, especially in the matter of art.

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:47.890
Art, we have come to talk about art, but what is your opinion about art?

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:49.890
Oh, I'm very happy that you ask me that.

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:50.910
I retire the question.

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:53.910
This man takes things very seriously, you can't ask him a simple question.

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:56.730
Tell me, where do you plan to install your new opera theater?

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:59.740
I thought I could build it near the Central Park.

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:00.000
to put it

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.030
- In the middle of the Central Park. - I could do it.

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:04.030
Sure, do it at night when no one sees you.

00:30:04.030 --> 00:30:06.030
Why don't you put it on the pond and lift the whole building up there?

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:08.030
Of course, that could intercept the flow of water.

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:10.030
But first of all, you have to think that art is art.

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:11.880
Although, on the other hand, water is water, right?

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:13.880
And the East is the East, and the West is the West.

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:15.880
And if you eat stewed potatoes and sauté it with apple sauce,

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:18.830
it tastes more like mackerel than if it were rhubarb.

00:30:18.830 --> 00:30:21.000
And now, tell me what you know.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:25.010
Well, I'd really like to tell you my opinions.

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:27.010
Great, I'll ask you some day. Remember it, okay?

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:30.000
Listen, can't you come to my office tomorrow at ten if I'm not there?

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:33.000
Ask Mr. Jameson, he's my secretary, and if he sees you, I'll take you to his office.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:36.000
We'll meet on Saturday at 3:00. No, it'll be better on Tuesday.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:38.000
I'm going to Europe on Monday. My name is Spalding.

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:40.000
I've always wanted to meet you, Mr. Chandler.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:42.000
Tell me, what do you think of the traffic problem?

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:44.000
What do you think of the marriage problem?

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.000
What do you think of the night when you go to bed, you brute?

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:48.000
Oh, well, I'll tell you, my friend.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.000
I'd rather not continue the discussion. Remember that the children are in front of you.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:52.950
Well, you'll see, my dear captain.

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:55.960
In the latest analyses, everything is a matter of money.

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:58.960
One cent today is not what it was ten years ago.

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:00.000
Well, I'm going very fast.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.340
Don't go any further, I'm going back. Today's cent is not the same as 15 years ago.

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:05.140
- Do you know what this country needs today? - What?

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:06.040
Seven cents.

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:09.960
We've been using the five-cent coin since 1492.

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:12.160
That represents about 100 years of routine.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.640
Why not give the seven-cent coin a chance?

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:16.900
So next year we'll have a eight-cent coin.

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:19.760
You can buy a three-cent newspaper and get the same coin back.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:22.840
With a carefully used coin, a family can live all their lives.

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:26.100
Captain Spaulding, I think it's a great idea.

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:27.330
- Oh, yeah? - Yeah!

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:29.510
Then it's worth nothing. Forget it.

00:31:30.320 --> 00:31:32.380
All right. Tell me, Captain Chandler...

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:34.660
Oh, excuse me. You're Spaulding, right?

